1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. d4 b5 6. Bb3 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 8.
Bd5 ({"Barden recommends the gambit} 8. c3 {" (Aitken)}) 8... Rb8 9. Bc6+ Bd7
10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. Qxd4 c5 ({"I like Black's 11th move.} 11... Nf6 {was played
in Hort-Keres, 1961" (Aitken)}) 12. Qe3 Nf6 13. O-O Be7 14. f3 O-O 15. c4 {
Aitken's comments at this point are close to illegible but the gist seems to
be that he didn't like White's 14th and 15th moves - JS} Qe6 16. cxb5 axb5 17.
Nc3 Rfd8 (17... b4 {looks better for Black. If} 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. exd5 Qd7 {
with a positional plus for Black (Saunders)}) 18. Ne2 d5 {"His ...d5 is a
forcing move that leads to a drawn ending." (Aitken's scorebook)} 19. Nf4 Qe5
20. Nxd5 Nxd5 21. exd5 Qxe3+ 22. Bxe3 Rxd5 23. Rfd1 Rbd8 24. Rxd5 Rxd5 {
Sources: Aitken Book 34; John Saunders scorebook. "I recall that we were the
first to finish and adjourn for a post-mortem, which was just as well because
the analysis room was tiny, with only one small table and chess set! I fondly
recall my chat with this affable old gent and cherish the memory. If anyone
had told us then that one day I would be involved in a project to preserve all
his chess games on a computer, I imagine we would both have been equally
astonished." (JS)} (24... Rxd5 {"The game would have continued} 25. a4 bxa4 26.
Rxa4 {with a draw" (Aitken)}) 1/2-1/2

